.TH WB\-PLAN 1 "" "watchbill"
.SH NAME
wb\-plan \- take a job in, as the text of its plan
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBwb\fR plan add [<name>] (\-F <file> | \-m <text>... | \-\-from <adapter> <number>)
            [\-\-source <address>] [\-\-level <level>] [\-\-hash\-only]
            \-F is \-\-file and \-m is \-\-message
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The plans of the jobs, kept in git (docs/revision.md, point 15). wb plan add takes a job in. Its text is given in one of three ways: \-F <file>, where \-F \- reads standard input, a pipe or a here\-document; \-m <text>, which may be given more than once and is joined and cleaned up of whitespace as git commit \-m does it; or \-\-from <adapter> <number>, which runs the pattern issue_text of watchbill.yml where its tracker: is the adapter, and names the job <adapter>/<number>. \-F is \-\-file and \-m is \-\-message, as in git. A file is read only where it is a plain file of 1 MiB or less, and a text that is empty or not UTF\-8 is refused. The text is stored as a git blob under its hash, and the name of the job is the ref refs/watchbill/<name>: a commit whose tree holds job.yml and the text under sources/<hash>. job.yml holds the first name, every name and, for each source, its hash, \-\-source <address> or none, the level, the git identity of the person and the time. With no name the job is named by the first 12 hex digits of the hash. A name may hold / (github/54); a name that git check\-ref\-format refuses, such as one with :, is refused. Other text under a name that is there adds a source, as a new commit after the one before; the same text again changes nothing; text that is already a job under another name gives that job this name too. Every name of the job is moved to its new commit together, or none is. \-\-level <level> is a level of the organisation's file no higher than the repository's, which is taken when none is given. \-\-hash\-only keeps the hash and the source and not the text, where the organisation's file allows it with jobs: hash_only: allowed. It says what it will write first, asks nothing of the network unless \-\-from runs a pattern, and only queues: it runs no prompt and starts no session.
.PP
Ends with 0 when the job is taken in or nothing changes, 1 when it is refused, 2 when it was used wrongly or the repository is not set up.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-F <file>, \-\-file <file>
Read the text from the file, a plain file of 1 MiB or less, or from standard input where the file is \-, as for a pipe.
.TP
.B \-m <text>, \-\-message <text>
The text in words; given more than once, each is a paragraph, cleaned up of whitespace as git commit \-m does it.
.TP
.B \-\-from <adapter> <number>
Read the text by the pattern issue_text of watchbill.yml, where its tracker: is the adapter, and name the job <adapter>/<number>.
.TP
.B \-\-source <address>
Where the text came from, kept in job.yml; none when it is not given.
.TP
.B \-\-level <level>
The level of the job: one of the organisation's levels, no higher than the repository's.
.TP
.B \-\-hash\-only
Keep the hash of the text and its source, not the text, where the organisation's file has jobs: hash_only: allowed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The job is taken in, or nothing changes.
.TP
.B 1
It is refused: a name git refuses, a level above the repository's, \-\-hash\-only not allowed, no organisation's file, or a text that is empty, over 1 MiB or not UTF\-8.
.TP
.B 2
It was used wrongly, no adapter is set up for \-\-from, or the repository is not set up.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/watchbill/organisation.yml
The levels, and whether \-\-hash\-only is allowed.
